A||N||1||Heisenhund||Information on cooling problems||||11||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||02-24-2001||11:45 AM||||Hi all,<P>Some of you may remember I had a problem with the temp in my 86 GT going from high peg, then dropping off & acting normally. Many suggested burping the cooling system (helped some), others said to use a lower temp thermostat (helped some). I also changed the water pump, gauge temp sensor and radiator cap. All these had little or no effect. Eventually the right head warped & caused all the problems associated with that.<P>After taking the engine apart to check evrything out, I also had to replace the crank & bearings (water contamination) and timing set. Anyway, it's back on the road....WITH THE SAME PROBLEM!!! So I says to myself, what could cause it to get so hot on initial run, but as soon as the stat opens once, it runs fine? Many of you probably know the answer to this, but it was so simple & unusual that I (and quite a few friends who have a more vast experience in automotive repair) missed it.<P>Yes the radiator has a pinhole leak. What's happening is the pressure isn't building up fast enough to prevent localized boiling around the heads. Hence the high temp. When I had a local shop press up the radiator, the leak became very apparent as did another leak in the coolant tube plug on the passenger side.<P>Just a note to all with mysterious cooling problems...CHECK EVERYTHING!!!!<P>Heis<P>P.S.<BR>Anybody got any good sources for radiators & coolant tube plugs?<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||02-24-2001||02:43 PM||||Keep an eye on the heater core. It may not be far behind the radiator if it's not holed already...||||reg||1
